body:
The workaround is a bit weird, and not immediately obvious (to me, but I'm a bit dim)...

Instead of using something like "index.json", convert to "index.js" (in your custom config) and then `module.exports` the JSON config object. Then you need to pass a fully qualified path to the _base.js config file, using `path.join(__dirname, '_base.js')`, as seen in the following snippet:

``` js
var pathJoin = require('path').join;

module.exports = {
  "extends": pathJoin(__dirname, "_base.js"),
  "rules": {
    "quotes": [2, "double"]
  }
};
```

Another, possibly prettier, solution/workaround may be something like `require.resolve()` (instead of `path.join()` and `__dirname`) in the custom ESLint config module:

``` js
module.exports = {
  "extends": require.resolve('eslint-config-node-style-guide'),
  "rules": {
    "quotes": [2, "double"]
  }
};
```

Results of the investigation:
If you directly reference a file inside the projects `.eslintrc` file then it works fine.

``` js
{
    "extends": "./config.js"
}
```

Now in case where you extend to a package and then inside that package you further extend to a file, then it cause the above error.

So your project `.eslintrc`:

``` js
{
    "extends": "ideal"
}
```

and inside ideal's js file

``` js
module.exports = {
    "extends": "./config1.js",
    "rules": []
};
```

this where you will get an error with`config1` in reference.

@pdehaan why don't you just:

``` js
var base = require("eslint-config-node-style-guide");

// make whatever changes you want
base.rules.semi = 0;

module.exports = base;
```

@nzakas I thought of that, but using JSON/YAML syntax felt a bit more consistent.
https://github.com/gulpjs/eslint-config-gulp/pull/6#issuecomment-150493224

Plus didn't know if it'd get awkward if I wanted to push new `extends` or whatever, where `extends` can be `undefined`, a string, or an array  and I'd be adding too much logic.
